// Certified payroll FILINGS — the act of filing a WH-347, as distinct from
// the numbers on it.
//
// Part of the multi-file Prisma schema in this folder.
//
// apps/web/lib/wh347.ts already builds page 1 of the federal form from
// TimeEntry rows and gets the grid right. It nonetheless reports
// `fileable: false` on every week, and two of the eleven reasons it lists
// are structural rather than missing data — nothing here can be derived
// from hours, so no amount of work on that module closes them:
//
//   1. Every WH-347 carries a SEQUENTIAL PAYROLL NUMBER for the project.
//      It is not a property of a week's hours; it is issued, once, when a
//      week is filed. Hence a counter.
//   2. PAGE 2, the Statement of Compliance, is signed under penalty of
//      perjury and states how fringe benefits were paid. It is an
//      ASSERTION BY A PERSON, not a computation. Nothing in the time data
//      knows whether the pension contribution went to an approved plan or
//      was paid in cash, because that is a fact about the company's
//      benefit arrangements, not about the hours.
//
// So this file holds what a person asserts and the app cannot compute.
// Everything derivable — hours, gross, fringe credit — stays derived from
// TimeEntry on every render and is deliberately NOT snapshotted here. A
// stored copy of a computed column can disagree with what it was computed
// from, which is the repo's standing rule, and a filing whose stored gross
// disagrees with its own time entries is the worst version of that.
//
// Announced in #prova-build before the push, per the working agreement,
// because it touches the shared schema. This shipped schema-only and the
// sentence here said so; both tables are now written by
// `apps/web/lib/actions/certifiedPayroll.ts` and read by
// `apps/web/app/(app)/jobs/[id]/certified-payroll/wh-347/page.tsx`, so
// that sentence is gone rather than left to be inherited as true.

/// How fringe benefits were paid, as page 2 of the WH-347 puts the
/// question. The three members are the form's own 4(a) / 4(b) split, not
/// a modelling choice — an agency reading the filing expects exactly
/// these, so the enum is the form's vocabulary rather than a tidier one.
///
/// APPROVED_PLANS is 4(a): paid into approved programs (pension, health
/// and welfare, vacation, training) for each hour worked. PAID_IN_CASH is
/// 4(b): paid directly to the worker in their cheque. BOTH exists because
/// a real union contractor is usually both at once — pension and H&W to
/// the trust fund, vacation in cash — and forcing that week into one box
/// would make the signer misstate it. There is deliberately no NONE and
/// no UNKNOWN: a signer who cannot say how fringes were paid has no
/// business signing, and a nullable enum here would let the app record a
/// perjury-bearing statement with the substantive claim left blank.
enum CertifiedPayrollFringeMethod {
  APPROVED_PLANS
  PAID_IN_CASH
  BOTH
}

/// Issues certified payroll numbers per job. Same reasoning as RfiCounter
/// and the five other counters in this schema: a number computed from the
/// filings that still exist (a count, or max+1) is freed again when one is
/// deleted, and the next filing reissues it. This row only increments.
///
/// The stakes are higher here than on an RFI. A payroll number is how an
/// awarding body checks that a contractor filed EVERY week — they read the
/// sequence and look for gaps. Two different weeks filed under number 7,
/// or a 7 that follows an 8, reads to the agency as a missing or falsified
/// payroll rather than as an application bug. `Invoice.number` in this
/// repo is the counter-example and the reason this comment is emphatic: it
/// uses max+1 and reissues numbers on delete.
///
/// Bumped inside the SAME transaction as the filing insert, by
/// `issueCertifiedPayrollNumber` in `apps/web/lib/actions/certifiedPayroll.ts`.
/// It takes the transaction client rather than `prisma` on purpose: a
/// filing whose insert fails — a duplicate week, i.e. a double click —
/// must not consume a number on its way out, because a hole in the
/// sequence reads to an agency exactly like a missing week.
model CertifiedPayrollFilingCounter {
  jobId      String @id
  job        Job    @relation(fields: [jobId], references: [id])
  lastNumber Int    @default(0)

  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt
}

/// One filed WH-347 for one job for one week — page 2 of the form, plus
/// the payroll number page 1 needs in its header.
///
/// This is an EVIDENCE RECORD in the sense the rest of the schema uses the
/// word (safety incidents, RFIs, submittals, contract documents): it says
/// that on a date, a named person asserted something to a government
/// agency under penalty of perjury. That framing decides every field
/// below, and it is why the model stores so little.
///
/// IDENTITY IS LOCKED AFTER CREATION: `jobId`, `weekEnding` and
/// `payrollNumber` are what the agency holds a copy of. Editing any of
/// them turns a filing into a different filing while keeping its
/// signature, which is the one thing this record exists to make
/// impossible. The lock is enforced IN THE ACTION, not by a database
/// trigger — #194 removed a trigger from `TimeEntry` for exactly that
/// reason: a trigger on a live table is invisible from the code, fires on
/// every write including migrations and backfills, and cannot produce the
/// `{ ok: false, error }` a form can render. So the lock IS the absence of
/// any write path: `certifiedPayroll.ts` exports one action, it only
/// `create`s, and `certifiedPayroll.test.ts` scans that file and fails the
/// build if an `update`, `upsert` or `delete` against this table ever
/// appears. Anyone adding one is being asked to build the amendment flow
/// instead.
model CertifiedPayrollFiling {
  id    String @id @default(cuid())
  jobId String
  job   Job    @relation(fields: [jobId], references: [id])

  /// The Saturday the payroll week ends, at UTC midnight, matching what
  /// `certifiedPayrollWeekWindow` computes and what `TimeEntry.date`
  /// stores. THE WEEK IS THE IDENTITY of a filing — see `@@unique` below.
  weekEnding DateTime

  /// Sequential per job, issued by CertifiedPayrollFilingCounter and never
  /// reissued. Goes in the "Payroll No." box of page 1's header, which
  /// `Wh347Header.payrollNumber` currently reports as blocking.
  payrollNumber Int

  /// The form's "final" checkbox — this is the last payroll for the
  /// contract. Not derived from job status: a job can sit at COMPLETE for
  /// months with a further payroll still owed, and the agency reads this
  /// box as a statement that no more are coming. Only the signer knows.
  isFinal Boolean @default(false)

  /// Page 2's substantive claim. See CertifiedPayrollFringeMethod.
  fringeMethod CertifiedPayrollFringeMethod

  /// Page 2's 4(c). Free text, because the form's own box is free text:
  /// the signer names the workers or classifications the 4(a)/4(b) answer
  /// above does NOT cover, and the shape of an exception is not something
  /// this app gets to constrain. Null means the signer claimed none, which
  /// is itself part of the sworn statement — it is not "not yet filled in",
  /// because a row only exists once somebody signed.
  exceptions String?

  /// Who signed. Required: an unsigned statement of compliance is not a
  /// statement of compliance, and a filing with a null signer would be a
  /// record of a perjury-bearing document with nobody attached to it.
  signedByUserId String
  signedBy       User   @relation(fields: [signedByUserId], references: [id])

  /// The date the person SIGNED, ENTERED by whoever recorded it — not the
  /// moment anybody clicked. Same call as
  /// `ContractDocument.executedSignedDate`, and for the same reason: the
  /// paper was signed on a date, and stamping `now()` would assert that
  /// the app watched it happen. A form signed Friday and entered Monday is
  /// ordinary; recording Monday would misdate a sworn document. Stored at
  /// UTC midnight like every other entered date here.
  signedDate DateTime

  /// The stamped audit companion to `signedDate`: when cstream was TOLD,
  /// as against when the signing happened. The two are separately useful
  /// precisely when they disagree — a signedDate that predates its own
  /// createdAt by a month is a backdated filing, and that is only visible
  /// because both are kept. Never shown as "the date of the filing".
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  /// ONE FILING PER WEEK PER JOB, and this constraint is the design, not a
  /// hygiene index.
  ///
  /// A filing has already been SENT TO AN AGENCY by the time anyone wants
  /// to change it. Letting a correction overwrite the row would destroy
  /// the record of what was actually sworn to and sent, leaving the app
  /// asserting that the corrected version is what the agency received —
  /// which is exactly backwards for a document whose whole value is being
  /// evidence of what was said. So a correction to a filed week is an
  /// AMENDMENT: a new record that supersedes this one and preserves it,
  /// the same way a change order amends a contract instead of editing it.
  ///
  /// THE AMENDMENT FLOW IS NOT BUILT. Nothing here carries a supersedes
  /// pointer or a revision number yet, so today this constraint simply
  /// means a week filed once cannot be filed again — the second attempt
  /// fails on the unique index and the action must say so rather than
  /// crash. Whoever builds amendments adds the pointer; the constraint
  /// above is written so that work is additive rather than a correction to
  /// a wrong shape.
  @@unique([jobId, weekEnding])
  @@index([jobId])
}
